17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123899 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "096c8f5d-b6c7-5d85-9db1-f7584ff8b60f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123900 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123900 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123900 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33e178fa-54f8-5974-8e7a-f5f421266282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123901 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123901 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123901 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5766bcb7-fe05-5602-945f-985df6475722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124191 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124191 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cf/cf01827dc23a26ed9d8d81333d735021d3a4eb494a434ba9fc38f1d110dfcc25.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124191 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124191 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <        \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <    \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n![](images/0a/0af9e8b587bbeb640ff9ec0cdc8197a9d99e8f5282772561b92ba74915cf1226.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point because there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124192 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124192 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/38057eabc400c888e287be8dbbd8b4de172b625fa5922188f99f71112213ef3b.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty, 2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124192 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124192 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is the set of points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* $\\delta{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\{ x : |  x- x_0| <           \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of $x_0$ is given by the open interval $( x_0-\\delta,  x_0+\\delta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e3aa5b8e0e93f7fda306a6f39c999af9a587614795316ce12fff8da2c9c2dd2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/38/389aa307a3c0c10b5d51b86bed6e05331fb9299379652f06e65a4a7b9f776ead.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c0992788a0ac7ce8cab2caabb41479ba2267e532412d1ef2c7115f56afb911c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124199 #edb/ref 1069 "e-11031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124199 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y > 4-2x\\big\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124199 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124199 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                   \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <               \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=4-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/8e/8e8e3296c59277b92eddddd3deacd7149fd9d7062aa05f4858e1846c3fb3238b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/07/077daf974625dd03a02b3f1c55e7b60c5a8c9686bc5515d78246bf7e1048e4cf.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ satisfy the equation $y = 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y > 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124200 #edb/ref 1069 "e-11032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124200 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1 \\bigg\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} =1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124200 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                  \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <              \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/d7/d70dec41d9d5d24d82d1efafd65f1898ffcd5794dca98ca46d486cd3be428a18.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1, 1)$ and $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/3e/3e99fed83e9e31f223bfb4c1a9df87aa0b54e7865c1bfbc5b175b684c7c3c58a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ satisfy the equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ Therefore, the point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186560996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124634 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124635 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124635 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124635 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186