 17592186045436] [17592186124795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98524e7d-f97e-5125-b634-0488b239406e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124795 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $A$ is not an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/39/39f7097f82f19d703208f9e4e8638e4d64aa417ad9cf4bc1f90092eb0c5e5639.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/3f/3ffa79d3052247aecd276c7794a498340acb66a1e74aeddcdd7800b1e9704506.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/f1/f116e950d99e0a00908c6c40a3c788cc450efa120843369e13f857cb084d8a1e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477596 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477597 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477598 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2x-4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The point $P(2,0)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(2,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(1,3)$ is an interior point of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8871194-baee-5d59-8d60-7a847fcf31fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124982 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2x-4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/b2/b2449c90a6565712b535df42840b8f874d6061ed240092b02ec5672152e125aa.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,0)$ and $Q(1,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/4a/4a176ab71239b0ec388d96f515b5e26767086ee37463119115dfe43ef190c506.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,0)$ satisfy the equation $y=2x-4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2x-4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449329 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449330 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449331 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b10043b-af03-5e4b-8ef7-b7ee2892ed6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477667 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477668 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477669 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/33/33d777f400b53d2272636fc9c465cf83c22932d8172c5d8a35bcdd2bdaae09a1.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- **II.** $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- **III.** $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e699ccb-274c-55bb-b36e-2ab5da35b953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125215 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/46/46670ef62c2afa2e5aa5c0eb4d1684493f35ab4f9a82aa5375994bf1cb2cb154.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125216 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0.5,0.5)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f3705f6-6b05-5981-9c91-c510c442e37e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125216 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0.5,0.5)$ and $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0.5,0.5)$  satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1061 41386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44c26f5a-57b1-5e3b-b126-e2f92e8f7bac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1061 41387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13578b7b-3533-5f2d-b6bc-c8214f0f163b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1061 41433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2201caf-3e1b-53b4-b391-8a27cfbbdce1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1061 41436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd03cb93-6f1c-502a-9888-3a2700a9c8a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1061 41912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23fe8ae7-b1b8-5490-93e8-49b20cd742f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125616 #edb/ref 1061 41916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125616 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc8051c9-6e2f-5ded-82a6-e902adddd2c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125616 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1061 44624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99fecfa4-9502-52e4-aff9-d84b074446bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1061 44625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b346783-d47e-5992-9033-dcf4627c7e39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1064 "A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0\\in \\mathbb R$ is the set of points that are \"close to\" $x_0.$ \n\nMore precisely, let's define a number $\\delta >0$, where $\\delta$ is usually very small.  A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0$ is the set of all points whose distance from $x_0$ is smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nIn one-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is an open interval $\\left(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta\\right),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04016c37be4c907eabc144db16eb757dff773907262ccd04941cf27ddd2e49d6.png)\n\nFor example, if we set $\\delta = 0.1,$ then the corresponding neighborhood of the point $x_0 = 2$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/03/0329bef56b8b903eb13510b7344fd7ca488d34c8d0c85c7648cebc8ecf7cfa40.png)\n\nNote that $x=1.9$ and $x=2.1$ are *not* included in this neighborhood since their distance from the point $x_0$ is *not* smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nWe can represent a neighborhood of $x_0$ using set notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x : | x- x_0| < \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nWe often refer to sets of this type as *open* neighborhoods since they contain all points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* (but not equal) to $\\delta.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1065 6818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b2ea7b59-5287-5d12-b53f-32a9254b8ba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1064 "In two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\mathbb R^2$ is a disc of radius $\\delta$ centered at $\\mathbf x_0$ that does not include the bounding circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nThe definitions of the interior and boundary points are the same as for the one-dimensional case. The only difference is that now a neighborhood of a point is a disc.\n\nFor example, consider the set $S$ and points $A,$ $B, C,$ and $D,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e037fd998c235ff7b721e7187d44814700ac5f41a256920f34f248c798c520.png)\n\nLet's classify each of these as interior or boundary points (or neither):\n\n- $A$ is an interior point since *there exists* a neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set.\n\n- $B$ is neither an interior nor a boundary point because *there exists* a neighborhood of $B$ that does not contain points from the set.\n\n- $C$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $C$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\n- $D$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $D$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\nNotice that although $C$ and $D$ are both boundary points, $C\\notin S$ and yet $D\\in S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1065 6825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bfda97a2-2fe7-5c9a-a067-dbf903ba2111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1067 "Interior and Boundary Points of Sets in the Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that $S$ is a set of real numbers. A point $x_0$ is called an **interior point** of $S$ if *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is *fully contained* inside $S.$ \n\nFor example, consider the set $S=[0,1)$ and the points $A$ and $B,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a12c60b6f60ca7c35162fb1ddf0d0d507fe80e73e621c451c21f0b882034ae21.png)\n\nThe point $A$ is an interior point of $S,$ but $B$ is not:\n\n- The point $A$ is an interior point of $S$ since there exists a neighborhood of $A$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n\n- The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $B$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$ In other words, any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nNote that neither $x=0$ nor $x=1$ are interior points. To see why, let's draw a neighborhood of each point:\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\n \n\nFrom here, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=0$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is also *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=1$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\nThe set containing all interior points of a set $S$ is called the **interior** of $S,$ and is denoted $\\textrm{int}\\,S.$ \n\nFor the set $[0,1),$ its interior is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,S = (0,1).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1065 7685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da6d3c21-fe0a-566e-a6e0-117f10988923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1067 "Interior Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1064 "A point $x_0$ is a **boundary point** of $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that *are* in $S$ *and* points that are *not* in $S.$ \n\nLet's consider the set $S=[0,1),$ the points $x=0, x=1,$ and neighborhoods of these points.\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\nBoth $x=0$ and $x=1$ are boundary points of $S{:}$\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\nThe set of all boundary points of a set $S$ is called the **boundary** of $S,$ and is denoted as $\\partial S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1)$ above, its boundary contains two points only, namely $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\partial S = \\{0,1\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1065 7686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "54b0ce40-c312-54e6-87c5-ba2b69af3dae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1067 "Boundary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448914 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448915 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448916 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12},\\,∂B={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(3,4)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n- **II.** The point $P(3,4)$ belongs to $B$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-3,6)$ is an interior point of $B$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f57db6a6-9edb-5f0f-98dd-62f31492deff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241043 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/adee860d39b4d9c4511db2b2f1ad6e7f46342cc474da47303af742be1cc911b3.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,4)$ and $Q(-3,6)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/21/2185e7129cd799eb1cbb888789566a406b1594dd5a649b9129bec491a1dce1c7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ does not satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $B.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $B$ and points that do not belong to $B.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450855 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450856 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450857 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7714139-ef1f-551b-b703-2555d562d417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278201 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/de/de056be6665a342dc8569e0db330743a96e08fa770798c94ec7dd0cc4e987d92.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,2)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/c6/c6c1237dfa5f8fed76bad01466c94442d27825c4edab7dbc92a99b177af4a0b6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $Q$ is an interior point of $A$ since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained in $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465243 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465244 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465245 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/43/43380e1382df5db66c4a9ab0ee385e98073da7fb684ccdf93a665e223fb1a554.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty ,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae427c1b-c208-5c4a-a164-5e046d8d2ea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278225 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/c6/c66edbc427dbd1e970a5c25422fbfca39296b0a90b6ca26dacb3280551105784.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/7b/7b8beea4c575e89b2c66f363271a90068e912422ad8991a333375f6894523a57.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a06851f25bf80edcc934fe7f341a6a43a065e744691164d46f77d116d4bf047.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465246 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465247 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465248 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186278226 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278226 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278226 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f4/f4ad8b759d3f2781b1dc6adf64e58a230218655249f21fdd8f6a85256855