lta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9acfd3e5e7d179904ba69d3473e5f8644055e777fa679e665cfb9199793d218.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $B$ does not belong to $M$ and $B$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $B.$\n\n![](images/74/7480978db0850ff426ed4747ee7dfb81781ad7222f97fca0d6106c800d99fcbf.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $C$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/4f/4fb2567d76963e586370ee771f359b3ba051941d650d9d1f0dc58f47bb770ab4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472244 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472245 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472246 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377655 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377655 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377655 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/94/94911d7be45c5572a4ce47ce95c81a66bf62